[UTCTime "16:01:26"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ I am officially retiring this opening, because it seems that it is just bad, at least at a high level.

I'll leave this study up as there are some good ideas and there is an important lesson to learn as to why the opening is bad.

The thinking of the opening is that it is worth being slow in development if you gain the option to castle and quickly make your king safe. But after lots of analysis in T0 and in DP, the move g6 seems to never hold up. }
3. h4 { The reason is simple, the plan of playing h4 then h5 is a good and effective plan, taking advantage of the move g6 which creates a target for the h-pawn. This will guarantee the white rook is activated, and weaken the g6 square.. This h4 h5 plan is good regardless of whether black has castled or not. It is even good regardless of what black has done with their dark squared bishop.
So, because white is given an effective plan that is good even if we delay castling, the opening has no advantages.
One last note, the g6 square itself is important to defend in general, in both T0 and DP, as it is on the diagonal to your king, the move g6 somewhat counterintuitively has the effect of making it easier for your opponent to target g6, so never play it.
In normal chess never play f6, in 5D chess never play g6 or f6 and also probably several other pawn moves. } *


