[UTCTime "16:18:11"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ In 5D chess, the pawns near the king are often difficult to move without making serious weaknesses. To me this means that developing pieces into the centre and skipping early pawn moves is viable, as enemy pawns are unlikely to harass them.

Another thing, is that in 5D chess you usually can't castle without getting a piece in front of your g pawn, or pushing your g pawn. But, castling remains a very strong move, so we fianchetto to prepare castling, and then castle.

This setup is very solid (notice that all squares near our king are very inaccessible to travels, and are also defended), and we are incredibly flexible in how we continue from here.

We will look at this opening from the perspective of black, as black is the side that can have trouble reaching this setup. }
 *


