[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/RyanFourLC"]
[FEN "rqbnkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RQBNKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.10.24"]
[UTCTime "16:28:15"]

{ This Chapter will go over a line where we spend one turn with a pawn move, which leads to us being too slow to develop, allowing the opponent to force us to give up going for our desired setup. }
1. c4 c5 { C5 is too slow, and doesn't really help us at all with our planned development. } 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. b3 Ne6 4. Bb2 g6 { Stubbornly trying to continue with the plan will lead to a very uncomfortable position } 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Nd5 { Now we cannot castle, as Nxe7 is checkmate. There is also the simple threat of Nxe7+ Kxe7 Ne5+, and we must give up the queen for 2 knights.
We'll show one sample line from here, but at this point it should be clear this is very undesirable.
We try capturing the knight. } 6... Nxd5 7. cxd5 { Capturing the bishop and allowing the queen onto the diagonal is certainly losing, so we'll try Nd4 } 7... Nd4 { This move also has serious flaws. A bishop or a queen travelling onto e7 on this board would lead to mate. } 8. e3 Nxf3+ 9. gxf3 Bxb2 10. Qxb2 f6 11. e4 Qe5 12. Be2 { White has just calmly forced trades and setup a bishop travel onto e7. } *


[Event "5D Chess Defended Pawn Ryan's Opening: 1. c4"]
[Date "2021.10.24"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "5D Chess Defended Pawn Ryan's Opening"]
[ChapterName "1. c4"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/PSMKzNf9/0aU33DpJ"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/RyanFourLC"]
[FEN "rqbnkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RQBNKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.10.24"]
[UTCTime "16:49:49"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

1. c4 { Develop towards the setup with the moves that leave you as flexible as possible. } 1... Nf6 { This knight always goes to f6, so this is the most flexible move. } (1... Ne6 { This undefends f7, and is generally less flexible than another move. }) (1... g6 { This is the most inflexible move that tries to reach the desired setup. }) (1... c5 { too slow, see the optional chapter }) (1... b5 { too slow, see the optional chapter }) 2. Nf3 { Keep going } 2... Ne6 { We still have not committed to the fianchetto, it is good to commit to the 3 turn plan of g6, Bg7, O-O as late as possible in case opponent does something that makes us want to deviate. } (2... g6 { We can't play Bg7 without undefending the weak e7 pawn, so g6 has no purpose yet. }) 3. b3 g6 { The opponent hasn't done anything crazy, so we can finally commit fully to the setup. } 4. Bb2 Bg7 5. Nc3 { Nd5 can be an annoying move, hitting the vulernable e7 pawn, prevent it. } 5... c6 (5... O-O { Now Nd5 attacks the e7 pawn, and to defend it we must play Re7, but this allows opponent to execute the very simple plan of Bc3, Qb2 lining up three attackers on your f6 knight. } 6. Nd5) 6. Ne4 { This is the opponent's only remaining way to try to pressure our position, but we don't need to worry about it, continue as normal. } 6... O-O { The remainder of this chapter, and the next 2 chapters will cover lines where the opponent captures the knight on f6. Capturing the knight on f6 is ineffective as far as I can tell, but defending can be tricky. } 7. Nxf6+ { A very important idea in this opening is that we are completely fine with getting doubled f-pawns, it opens the e-file for our use, and having a pawn blocking paths to your king is good.
Also our dark squared bishop is our biggest asset. } 7... exf6 8. Bc3 { Opponent is desperately trying to continue to attack us, but this is actually a big mistake. They are planning to try to form a bishop-queen battery and take on f6, but they have a weakness in their position, try to find a move that lets you target that weakness. } 8... a6!! { Setting up Qa7 and then travelling back onto the earliest turn where opponent undefended the f2 pawn. } (8... b5 { This move is very reasonable, to set up Qb6, but there is a better square to target f2 from. }) (8... Qd6 { This move is good, it prepares to defend f6, and also prepares to go to c5 to target f2. But there is a better square to target f2 from. }) (8... a5 { This move is just as good as the move a6, which is the intended move. }) (8... Qc7 { A reasonable move, to try to play Qb6 and target f2, but there is a better square to target f2 from. }) 9. Bd4 { The only move that stops Qa7, but now we can get rid of our opponent's best piece. } 9... Nxd4 10. Nxd4 { Now go in for the kill. } 10... Qf4 { Threatening a mating travel onto f2, and attacking the knight. } *


