[FEN "rqb2rk1/pp1pppbp/2p1nnp1/8/2P1N3/1P3N2/PB1PPPPP/RQ2KB1R w KQ - 2 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.10.25"]
[UTCTime "19:53:01"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

7. Bc3 { Capturing our knight immediately doesn't work well, so opponent may try to line up the queen-bishop battery before capturing.
We have many ideas that we learned from previous chapters, a6 to play Qa7 and counter-attack f2, d5 to force the knight to make a decision, and Qf4 to pressure the opponent's knight.
Which idea works best? } 7... d5 (7... a6 { Opponent can counter this with c5, they even have a check with Nxf6 do completely close the triagonal, so this doesn't quite work. }) (7... a5 { Opponent can counter this with c5, they even have a check with Nxf6 do completely close the triagonal, so this doesn't quite work. }) (7... Qf4 { This move is probably fine, but it is too complex for the purposes of this study. }) 8. cxd5 cxd5 9. Nxf6+ { Opponent has to capture now, or retreat with it and lose all of their pressure. } 9... exf6 10. Qb2 { When we played d5 we didn't just want to force the knight to capture, that pawn has a grand purpose. } 10... d4 11. Bxd4 { Opponent can't take the pawn with knight, as we've seen before Qf4 is deadly. } 11... Nxd4 12. Qxd4 { Opponent's king is far less safe than ours, but we should take one more precaution to safeguard our one weakness. } 12... h5 { We will stop here as all danger is gone from our position.
The plans from here are very simple:
Play Bg4, trade the knight and play f5 to activate the dark squared bishop.
Put rooks on the open e/d files.
Stockfish thinks this position is completely equal, which should mean that in 5D chess where king safety is more relevant, black is better. } *


