3. d4 { Now we are rewarded for playing the most flexible knight moves, opponent has opened a line to their king, try to undermine the enemy pawns and open up the queen. } 3... b5 { d5 here is not possible for white as xc4 poses a checkmate threat with Qb4, and wins a pawn.
c5 is not concerning to us, as then they can never play d5 to harass our knight } (3... c5 { d5 here is quite annoying, but this line is probably playable for black }) 4. b3 { Opponent tries to hold on to their plan of playing d5, but now we can strike back with our queen. } 4... Qb6 (4... bxc4 { Doing this to force a queen trade is fine. }) 5. Bd2 { Opponent has to stop the Qa5 mate threat, and now we can win a pawn.
In general d4 is not a troubling move so long as you quickly attack their d4/c4 pawns and harass the open diagonal to their king. } *


